--- result ---
  ArgoCD Application rollouts-demo    Synced / Healthy
  Terraform now owns                  helm_release.rollouts_demo_app[0]  (the
                                      ownership RECORD) — not the workload
  helm release rollouts-demo          revision 8, UNCHANGED (the baseline check's
                                      whole point)
  Rollout uid                         1aab334a-… unchanged  (not recreated)
  Service uid / clusterIP             b117e477-… / 10.96.70.166 unchanged
  selfHeal                            scaled 4 -> 2, restored to 4 within ~40s

--- BUT: adoption was NOT a no-op, and the preflight did not predict it ---
Every check passed and a canary still rolled: a new ReplicaSet appeared and the
Rollout paused at 50%. Diffing the old and new pod templates:

  .spec.containers[0].ports      ABSENT -> [{containerPort: 8080, name: http}]
  .spec.containers[0].resources.requests
                                 ABSENT -> {cpu: 5m, memory: 16Mi}

The handoff was not at fault. The LIVE Rollout had silently lost those fields,
so ArgoCD re-asserting git's desired state was a genuine change — the adoption
REPAIRED drift rather than causing it.

Cause, self-inflicted earlier in the session: `kubectl patch --type=merge` on a
container list. `--type=merge` is a JSON merge patch (RFC 7386), which REPLACES
arrays wholesale — a patch carrying only `name` and `image` drops every other
field of that container. Strategic merge would have merged by the `name` key;
`--type=merge` does not. Nothing looked wrong: the pods had no readiness probe,
so they reported Ready while the Service's named `targetPort: http` had nothing
to resolve against.

--- the fix: a 5th preflight check ---
`live-matches-rendered` diffs `helm get manifest` against the live objects and
lists every field the live object no longer carries. NON-BLOCKING on purpose:
adoption re-asserts the manifest either way, so drift is not dangerous by
itself. It is dangerous because unexpected churn reads as "the handoff broke
it" and sends you reaching for the rollback.

One-directional: extra fields in the live object are server-side defaults
(clusterIP, status, …), and the engine's own `argocd.argoproj.io/tracking-id`
is filtered — a check that fires on every single handoff gets ignored.

  [ok] live-matches-rendered: live objects match the rendered manifest —
       adoption should be a true no-op

--- trying to demonstrate the new check live, and what that showed instead ---
Re-running the exact `--type=merge` footgun and immediately re-reading the
object: ports and resources were gone. Seconds later the preflight reported NO
drift — because ArgoCD's selfHeal had already put them back.

That is not the check failing; it is the handoff working. Post-adoption, this
class of drift cannot persist. The check matters for the state the preflight is
actually run in: TF-owned, with nothing continuously reconciling. Pinned by
unit guards instead (tests/test_handoff_preflight.py::TestLiveMatchesRendered),
including the exact rendered/live pair that caused the churn.

--- remaining ---
loki / tempo / pa hold data; a volume snapshot mechanism is still required
before those three (kind has no CSI snapshotter by default). rollouts-demo was
chosen first precisely because a failed adoption there costs uptime, not data.
